.TH SHOAL-SANDBOX-EXEC 1 "July 2026" "Shoal 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shoal-sandbox-exec \- run a process under Shoal child-only OS controls
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shoal-sandbox-exec
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Applies requested process limits, filesystem policy, and network denial,
then replaces itself with COMMAND while preserving its standard streams.
Filesystem options are repeatable.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-deny-net
Deny network access in the child.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu-seconds " N"
Limit CPU time to a positive number of seconds.
.TP
.BI \-\-memory-bytes " N"
Limit the child address space to a positive byte count.
.TP
.BI \-\-read " PATH"
Permit reads below PATH.
.TP
.BI \-\-write " PATH"
Permit writes below PATH.
.TP
.BI \-\-delete " PATH"
Permit deletion below PATH.
.TP
.BR \-h , " \-\-help"
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V , " \-\-version"
Print the version and exit.
.SH OUTPUT
On successful exec, COMMAND owns stdout and stderr.
Setup failures are reported on standard error before COMMAND starts.
.SH ERRORS
Missing commands, malformed positive limits, unknown options, unavailable enforcement,
and exec failures are rejected before unconfined fallback can occur.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
shoal-sandbox-exec --deny-net --read . -- cargo test
shoal-sandbox-exec --memory-bytes 536870912 -- ./worker
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
After a successful exec, COMMAND determines the status.
Setup or exec failures return 126.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shoal (1),
.BR shoal-kernel (1),
.BR shoal-landlock-helper (1)
